  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "Is Removing an Old Shower the Best Path Before Installing a New Enclosure?",
    "text": "Is Removing an Old Shower the Best Path Before Installing a New Enclosure?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Question: Is completely removing an old shower enclosure necessary before putting in a new shower system?\n\nAnswer: In almost every circumstance, yes. While some products claim you can install an enclosure over old tile or existing pans, full removal is the standard approach for a long-lasting bathroom renovation. \n\nWhen you strip out the old tile, surround panels, and pan down to the bare framing, you gain visual access to the structural framing, subflooring, and plumbing lines. Over years of use, tiny grout hairline fractures or failed caulk joints often permit moisture to seep into wall cavities. Leaving old materials in place traps that hidden moisture, giving mold a dark environment to spread and allowing subfloors to rot undetected. Furthermore, a new pan or tile surround requires a plumb, square, and completely level backing surface to ensure proper drainage slope and prevent cracked grout joints later on. Full demolition also allows you to update old valves, verify drain alignment, and replace deteriorating supply lines.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf your existing enclosure has cracked tiles, loose wall panels, or persistent moisture smells, tearing it out down to the studs is essential. When structural repairs or plumbing shifts are involved, municipal building guidelines often require inspections before new walls are installed. \n\nHH Remodeling LLC handles full shower tear-outs, subfloor repairs, and custom bathroom carpentry across the Denver metro area. Call owner Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to review your bathroom remodeling needs.",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
